INT. RELAY STATION KESTREL - ORBITAL CORRIDOR - NIGHT CYCLE

A narrow tube lined with cable trays and amber emergency strips. Frost on the viewport. Beyond it, the planet turns, enormous, blue-grey, indifferent.

MIREILLE OSEI-TANAKA, thirties, in a thermal coverall, floats in the corridor with a handheld recorder clamped in her teeth. She pulls herself along a rail, one-handed, a tablet strapped to her forearm.

The tablet blinks red: LIFE SUPPORT RESERVE 14 PERCENT.

She stops. Takes the recorder from her mouth.

MIREILLE
Log. Day sixty-one. Oxygen is the problem now. Not the comms, not the hull. Oxygen.

She presses a key on the tablet. A recorded voice plays through the speaker, her own, crisp and unhurried.

MIREILLE (V.O.)
(recording)
Day nine. Everything nominal. I've started a plant. I'm calling it Gerald.

Mireille almost smiles. She pushes off toward the end of the corridor, drifting past a row of sealed hatches.

MIREILLE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recording)
Day twenty-two. Still nominal. Gerald is dying. I think I overwatered him.

A hatch light flickers. She grabs the frame, braces, and keys a code. The hatch opens on a dark hydroponics bay, lit only by one withered stalk in a clear tube.

MIREILLE
(to the plant)
Hello, old friend.

The tablet chirps. Another recording plays, closer, rougher.

MIREILLE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recording)
Day fifty. If anyone is hearing this, I have sixteen days of air and a very sick shrub. Please send a ship. Please send a ship that knows how to fix a shrub.

She floats to the tube. Touches the glass.

MIREILLE
Sixty-one.

She twists a valve. A pale mist hisses into the tube. The stalk shivers, and a single bud, no bigger than a grain of rice, opens.

Mireille stares at it. Then at the tablet. Then back at the bud, and without a word she begins pulling cables free from the wall panel, humming under her breath, working faster.

MIREILLE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recording)
Day sixty-one. Gerald lives.
